{"records":[{"id":"fmrnybgxb9","title":"NutsBD: An image dataset for classification of Nuts","description":"There are ten types of Nut: almond, Brazil nut, cashew, Thai peanut, Vietnam cashew, hazelnut, macadamia, peanut, pistachio, and walnut.\nThe dataset comprises 1742 images, with over 100 images for each nut type.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-20T17:07:32.169Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fmrnybgxb9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fmrnybgxb9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohammad Manzurul","last_name":"Islam","orcid":"0000-0002-3008-081X"},{"first_name":"Rafid ","last_name":"Mubarrat ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dipraj ","last_name":"Malaker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sumaiya ","last_name":"Tabassum ","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Image Classification"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhthnd824r","title":"Discerning of anti microbial peptides (AMPs) from whole genome sequenced nut crops","description":"This dataset contains BLAST as well as MACREL predicted AMPs (identified from whole genome sequenced nut crops  namely, almond, Chinese chestnut, pecan nut, pistachio nut, walnut) with e-value of <1 and identity percentage ≥ 50. Besides, the data on categorization of the predicted AMPs from the selected whole genome sequenced nut crops based on the key physicochemical properties and the general AMP database (retrieved from DRAMP database) used for blasting against crop protein  and MACREL output file were also provided.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-31T09:24:01.237Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhthnd824r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhthnd824r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vijayalakshmi","last_name":"K","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Senthilkumar","last_name":"S","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manivannan","last_name":"S","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shelvy","last_name":"S","orcid":"0000-0002-9525-8957"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Antimicrobial Peptide","Horticultural Crops","Nut Crops"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hwhctkv24s","title":"Systemic Health Effects of Areca Nuts with Particular Emphasis on Carcinogenesis – A Review | IJB 2021","description":"Areca (betel) nut (Areca catechu) is regarded as one of the most addictive masticatory substances in the world, with approximately 600 million users globally. Extensively grown and found in India, China, and Indonesia; the areca nut is also native to many other countries of the South and Southeast Asia. Its usage and beneficial effects have been reviewed for centuries. The systemic effects associated with areca nut usage have been in the limelight for many years. This review provides a narrative insight to assess and collate published data that reports the harmful effects of areca nut. An electronic search amassed literature from the NCBI and Google Scholar databases with the help of keywords. Various studies on the correlation of areca nut and its effect on the oral health, metabolism and the incidence of cancer were selected. Its usage is reported to be associated with cancers of the oral cavity, esophagus, pharynx, liver and has been declared a human group 1 carcinogen by the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC). It is also associated with the incidence of cardiovascular disease and chronic kidney disease. Hence, there is a dire need to identify the role of the areca nut as a causative agent of cancer and several other health disorders. Clinical evidence has indicated and proved that the use of areca nut causes several types of head and neck cancers, as to effects on intracellular levels of GSH, and on the tumor-suppressing gene, P53 is quite evident clinical evidence has indicated and proved that the use of areca nut causes several types of head and neck cancers, as to effects on intracellular levels of GSH, and on the tumor-suppressing gene, P53 is quite evident.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-01-10T17:17:35.927Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hwhctkv24s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hwhctkv24s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Saba ","last_name":"Shamim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"INNSPUB","last_name":"JOURNALS","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Biotechnology","Peer Review"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sb9z344rrm","title":"Supplementary data - Does the presence of conspecifics affect nutcracking behavior? ","description":"Although social learning in tool use among capuchin monkeys (Sapajus spp.) has been widely studied, the influence of conspecific presence on the nut-cracking behavior of adult capuchins remains largely unexplored despite evidence that capuchins are sensitive to conspecific presence in foraging contexts. We aimed at deepening our understanding of social presence effects on tool-use activity related to social context by testing whether adult tufted capuchin monkeys’ nut-cracking behavior varies with conspecific presence, and discussed how social rank and age influenced nut-cracking behavior. Studying a semi-free group of tufted capuchins at Tietê Ecological Park (TEP), São Paulo, Brazil we analyzed 80 nut-cracking episodes involving five proficient adults (three males, two females). Of these, 44 episodes involved at least one conspecific present. We ran Generalized Linear Mixed Models (GLMMs) with a Gamma distribution and logarithmic link to assess three predictors: (1) presence of conspecifics, (2) number of conspecifics, and (3) conspecific age class, testing against two response variables: nut-cracking behaviors occurrence rate and duration. Kruskal-Wallis tests assessed individual differences. Overall, our results indicate that nut-cracking behavior in adult capuchin monkeys is generally stable, even in the presence of conspecifics, suggesting that social context does not significantly affect performance. However, nut-cracking duration differed between two females during episodes involving conspecifics. We propose that individual and social factors interact to shape engagement in nutcracking, and that assessing the quality of social interactions during episodes may help clarify how individual characteristics and social dynamics jointly influence tool use.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-01T16:59:10.481Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sb9z344rrm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sb9z344rrm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bruna","last_name":"de-Sá","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2v772rmx5w","title":"Supplementary data - Nut-cracking behavior of tufted capuchin monkeys varies with social context and individual proficiency ","description":"Understanding how individuals influence each other is pivotal to the study of socially mediated behaviors. In social transmission processes, both demonstrators and observers influence one another's behavior and subtle changes in behavioral patterns may create learning opportunities, impacting manipulative activities such as tool use. However, social learning studies have historically focused on observers, particularly young individuals. Although tool-use behavior among capuchin monkeys (Sapajus spp.) have been widely studied, the influence of conspecific presence on the nut-cracking behavior of adult capuchin monkeys remains largely unexplored. Thus, we evaluated whether there was variation in the nut-cracking behavior of adult tufted capuchin monkeys associated with social context. Studying a semi-free group of capuchins at Tietê Ecological Park, São Paulo, Brazil, we analyzed 95 nut-cracking episodes involving six adults (four males and two females), 49 of which involved at least one conspecific. We fitted Generalized Linear Mixed Models to assess the effects of (1) social context, (2) number of conspecifics, (3) conspecific age class, (4) individual proficiency, and their interactions on both the occurrence rate of nut-cracking behavior and the proportion of episode time spent nut-cracking. Our results revealed variation in the occurrence rate of nut-cracking behavior associated with social context and individual proficiency, with less proficient individuals displaying higher rates during episodes with conspecifics present. We found no differences associated with the number or conspecific age class. These findings highlight that variability in individual behavior is not restricted to young individuals, suggesting association between individual proficiency on tool use and social context.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-08T11:15:34.593Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2v772rmx5w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2v772rmx5w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bruna","last_name":"de-Sá","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tool Use","Primatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r46c6bpfpf","title":"Coffee and Cashew Nut Dataset","description":"Our research focuses on using machine learning and drone technology to improve yield estimation in agriculture. We introduce the \"Coffee and Cashew Nut Dataset,\" containing 6,086 images with annotations of coffee and cashew nut crops. We collected this data from different coffee and cashew growing sites across Uganda through geo-tagged and time stamped drone imagery, capturing details about crop type and fruit maturity.\n\nWe meticulously curated and annotated the drone image dataset, involving agricultural experts for validation. This high-quality dataset is publicly available for various machine learning experiments.\n\nOur dataset has significant implications, offering precise, rapid, and cost-effective yield estimation solutions for farmers. It supports the development of machine learning models for crop classification, detection, and yield estimation, especially when combined with vegetation indices.\n\nThe dataset enables the creation of machine learning systems to assist farmers in refining yield estimates and sales predictions by detecting and counting unripe, ripe, and spoilt fruits. It's a valuable resource for advancing agriculture in Uganda and other African nations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-10T12:52:28.139Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r46c6bpfpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r46c6bpfpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joyce","last_name":"Nakatumba-Nabende","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Katumba","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rahman","last_name":"Sanya","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeremy","last_name":"Tusubira","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sudi","last_name":"Murindanyi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gloria","last_name":"Namanya","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ann","last_name":"Nabiryo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Lacuna fund","grant_id":"19497.51"}],"categories":["Computer Vision","Machine Learning","Agriculture Industry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pv6vxb6mnt","title":"Effect of nuts on lipid profile and inflammatory biomarkers in atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials","description":"Background: Nut-enriched diets positively impact lipid and inflammatory profile biomarkers in meta-analyses in the context of primary cardiovascular prevention. However, primary studies on secondary cardiovascular prevention are scarce and controversial.\nObjectives: This systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to assess the effect of nut supplementation on lipid and inflammatory profile biomarkers in individuals with atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, and the frequency of adverse events. \nMethods: Six databases were used for research: PubMed, EMBASE, BVS, Cochrane Library, Web of Science, and ClinicalTrials.gov, until February 2023, with no language restrictions. The Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions methodology and the PICO strategy were used. We performed random-effects meta-analyses to compare nut-enriched diets vs. control diets for pre-post intervention changes. The Grading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and Evaluation (GRADE) system assessed the evidence's certainty, and the protocol was registered in the PROSPERO database under the identifier CRD42020163456.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-10T12:51:14.2Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pv6vxb6mnt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pv6vxb6mnt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gustavo","last_name":"Waclawovsky","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aline","last_name":"Marcadenti","orcid":"0000-0003-1994-4610"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s83f5y9ntt","title":"DUB3 promotes BET inhibitor resistance and cancer progression through deubiquitinating BRD4. Jin et al","description":"The bromodomain and extra-terminal domain (BET) protein BRD4 is emerging as a promising anticancer therapeutic target. However, resistance to BET inhibitors often occurs, and it has been linked to aberrant degradation of BRD4 protein in cancer. Here, we demonstrate that the deubiquitinase DUB3 binds to BRD4 and promotes its deubiquitination and stabilization. Expression of DUB3 is transcriptionally repressed by the NCOR2-HDAC10 complex. The NCOR2 gene is frequently deleted in castration-resistant prostate cancer patient specimens, and loss of NCOR2 induces elevation of DUB3 and BRD4 proteins in cancer cells. DUB3-proficient prostate cancer cells are resistant to the BET inhibitor JQ1 in vitro and in mice, but this effect is diminished by DUB3 inhibitory agents such as CDK4/6 inhibitor in a RB-independent manner. Our findings identify a previously unrecognized mechanism causing BRD4 upregulation and drug resistance, suggesting that DUB3 is a viable therapeutic target to overcome BET inhibitor resistance in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-13T19:55:44.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s83f5y9ntt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s83f5y9ntt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunoprecipitation","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"4n4rjp9pdk","title":"Synergistic interactions between tocol and phenolic extracts from different tree nut species against human cancer cell lines","description":"The phenolic compounds and tocols from tree nuts have an effect to reduce cell viability, however the combination of both extracts of pecan nut, walnut and emory oak acorn showed a synergistic pharmacological interaction that significantly reduced the viability of cancer cells such as MCF7 and HeLa","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-01-12T15:37:51.364Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4n4rjp9pdk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4n4rjp9pdk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jazmin ","last_name":"Stevens Barron","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tocopherol","Phenolic Compound","Population Viability Analysis","Tree Nut"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"92g33dsbwd","title":"Vegan_datasets","description":"These are dietary classification datasets we collected from ChatGPT and validated with three dietary experts (dietitians) based on two health conditions (normal and obesity) and three classes of allergies (gluten allergy, nut allergy and no allergy). ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-17T17:32:58.855Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/92g33dsbwd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/92g33dsbwd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Halleluyah","last_name":"Aworinde","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Grace","last_name":"Ataguba","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Munachiso","last_name":"Okenyi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henry Kosi","last_name":"Clinton","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rita","last_name":"Orji","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Nutrition","Health","Large Language Model"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":144439,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":181,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":144439,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":143611,"restricted":73,"embargoed":755},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2363},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1393},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":710},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":480},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":456},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":399},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":320},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":141765},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1913},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":44},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":903},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":845},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":538},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":376}]}}